/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   LUCKY SOUNDS · L3 RHYTHM
   Where the beat comes from. Set aside before starting: sixteen random
   booleans; a kick on 1 and 3 with a snare on 2 and 4; Euclidean patterns for
   everything; quantising every onset to the grid.
   What is here instead: a metric weight hierarchy that says which positions
   in a bar can carry an accent, necklaces (Euclidean AND the named
   traditional patterns) that can be rotated, polymeter, and microtiming, so
   the grid is a reference the parts play AROUND rather than sit on.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── Euclidean necklaces ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Bjorklund's algorithm: distribute k hits over n steps as evenly as the
   integers allow. Almost every traditional world rhythm turns out to be one
   of these — E(3,8) is the tresillo, E(5,8) the cinquillo, E(7,16) a common
   bell pattern — which is why this is a generator of real rhythms rather
   than of plausible-looking ones. */
