/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   KNOCK · K3 THE GRAMMARS
   Seventeen drum languages, written down. Patterns and grooves are nobody's
   property — you cannot own a rhythm, only a recording of one — so these are
   the real figures, PLAYED here by synthesized drums, with the lineage named
   on every style. What IS ours is the grammar around each figure: which
   ornaments a style allows, what its fills are, when it drops out, and how
   its clock breathes.

   Feel numbers are measured, not vibed (research pass, 2026-08-26):
   · MPC swing (Roger Linn's own math): only the 2nd 16th of each 8th pair is
     delayed, by (swing%-50)/100 x (30000/bpm) ms. In this engine's units the
     delay is in STEPS: swingSteps = (pct-50) * 0.04.
   · Funky Drummer: beat 2 lags ~2.8% of a beat, beat 3 ~1.8%, beat 4 barely
     (Freeman & Lacey 2002); lateness is forgiven, earliness reads as error
     (Fruhauf 2013). The limb tables lean late, never early, except hats.
   · Ghost notes sit at 0.3-0.45 of the accent velocity (-12..-18dB).
   · Dilla: the KICK is the erratic voice — snare and hats hold the grid
     while kicks anticipate by MPC-tick multiples (~7ms at 96bpm).
   · Dual swing (Frane 2017): a break can carry hats at 1.2:1 while the kick
     swings 1.5:1 — so swing here is PER LANE (kickSwing overrides).
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

function kswing(pct) { return (pct - 50) * 0.04; }

