/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   LUCKY SOUNDS · L4 ENGINE
   The thing that turns a score into samples. One engine serves both hosts:
   the AudioWorklet that plays the page and the offline renderer that writes a
   file. That is deliberate — two code paths would eventually disagree, and
   the file would stop being what the listener heard.

   Timing is SAMPLE ACCURATE. Events carry a sample index, the render loop
   splits its block at every event boundary, and a note starts on the sample
   it was written for rather than at the top of the next 128-sample block.
   At 48kHz a block is 2.7ms; quantising every onset to it is audible as a
   loose, slightly flammed feel — the thing that makes browser sequencers
   sound like browser sequencers.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── per-patch loudness trims ──────────────────────────────────────────────
   Measured, not guessed. Thirteen engines built from different physics have
   no reason to arrive at the same loudness, and a mixer that assumes they do
   spends its life fighting them. Each number is the gain that puts that patch
   at a common reference RMS across three octaves; they are baked in rather
   than measured at load because measuring costs seconds of silence at start.
   Regenerate with tools/calibrate.js after any DSP change. */
