/* ── auto-levelling ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Worlds arrive spread across nine decibels — a five-part ballad against a
   nine-part drop — and on an endless scroll that means reaching for the
   volume every time you move.

   The obvious fix was to PREDICT the level from the score: how many parts,
   how loud each is trimmed, how much of the time each sounds. It was written,
   fitted against twenty-six rendered worlds, and it explained almost nothing
   — R-squared 0.004, a correlation of essentially zero. A mix's loudness is
   decided by what the glue compressor does, by how the parts overlap in time
   and by where they sit in the spectrum, and none of that is legible in a
   note list. The predictor is gone.

   What is here instead is what broadcast actually does: MEASURE IT. Gated,
   K-weighted loudness is integrated as the piece plays, and the gain converges
   on it — quickly for the first few seconds, then slower and slower, so that
   by the time the first section is over the correction has settled and stops
   moving. That ordering matters: a leveller that kept adapting would flatten
   the tension curve the arrangement was built around, quietly undoing the
   one thing that makes the piece rise and fall. */
var LU_TARGET = 0.105;                          /* about -19.6 dBFS, gated */
